WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.304
Fu Jian, emperor of Former Qin, commanded an army said to number a million, in majestic might.

00:00:08.654 --> 00:00:14.822
As far as the eye could see, tents and soldiers stretched without end.

00:00:15.172 --> 00:00:20.476
Before setting out, he proudly boasted to his generals.

00:00:20.826 --> 00:00:25.650
He puffed out his chest, his voice loud and ringing, full of confidence.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.048
He said: "If every one of my soldiers throws his whip into the Yangtze, we can cut off the river's flow!"

00:00:32.398 --> 00:00:39.622
But the Yangtze flows swift and strong — whips thrown in did not raise even a single splash.

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:45.612
The great river still rolled onward, not stopping in the slightest.

00:00:45.962 --> 00:00:51.266
Fu Jian's underestimation of the enemy planted the seeds of defeat for his great army.

00:00:51.616 --> 00:00:56.872
"Throwing whips to stop the river" became an idiom describing arrogant overconfidence — overestimating one's own strength.

00:00:57.222 --> 00:01:01.782
This story reminds us that we must not be too proud and arrogant in what we do.

